    "insert": "The exposé successfully includes all the important components and provides a clear explanation of the relevance of the problem. However, it does not explain why the study specifically targets CS and ME B.Sc. programs at TUDA. Given the scope's specificity, an explanation would enhance clarity and contextual understanding.\n\nThe gaps in existing literature are identified effectively. The research question aligns with the problem statement, but it is not positioned against the existing literature. This is understandable given the specificity of the research question, which makes positioning challenging. Despite this, the research question avoids ambiguity in terminology and is clear.\n\nTheories and concepts are introduced in an understandable manner, albeit at a very high level without much depth. For instance, the concept of INSPIRE is mentioned but not explained, leaving the reader seeking more clarity. While the choice of methodology is not explicitly justified based on the research question, it is reasonably apparent why it was selected. Additionally, the author does outline how the methods will provide answers to the research questions.\n\nDifficulties, risks, and limitations are adequately addressed, but ethical concerns, which could offer interesting insights, are notably absent. The schedule presented seems realistic and achievable, although the workload appears challenging. Major tasks and milestones are accounted for, providing a comprehensive roadmap for the study.\n\nThe overall argumentation is partly convincing but leaves some gaps. For instance, the research question's specificity is never fully justified. In contrast, some sections, such as the State of the Art (SOTA) or Theoretical Framework (TF), are overly general, lacking the specificity needed to match the focus of the research question. While examples are provided in the motivation and one instance of INSPIRE is mentioned, additional examples in the SOTA and TF sections would enhance understanding. Illustrations, however, do not seem necessary at this stage.\n\nLastly, some stylistic improvements could be made. The text includes subheadings, but a continuous format may be more cohesive. The exposé's title is very generic compared to the specificity of the research question; adapting the title to better reflect the focus would improve alignment. Additionally, the tone occasionally shifts to a colloquial style, which feels inconsistent with the professional nature of the rest of the text.\n"
